window.WIKI_INDEX = [{"t":"BMWeb Wiki","u":"index.html","x":""},{"t":"BMWeb Wiki § Where the facts come from","u":"index.html#evidence","x":"Every claim carries a tag saying how it was established. Where two sources disagree, the higher one in this list wins. Tag Means car Seen on a real car or module, usually in a wire log. emulator Measured by running the unit's own firmware in an emulator, against BMWeb or a scripted tester. firmware Read from the disassembly of the named software release, not yet seen on a car. BMW data Read from BMW's own data files: the diagnostic descriptions (SGBD), coding files and scripts, run offline where they are programs. field report Reported by another owner testing on their car. Plausible, not reproduced by us. Byte numbers count from 0, and bit 0 is the least significant bit. A telegram is written as hex bytes as they go on the wire."},{"t":"BMWeb Wiki § Before you use any of this","u":"index.html#safety","x":"These pages describe how the units behave, not a procedure that is safe on your car. A write to a control unit can leave it unable to start. Read the unit's own data back before you change it, keep a copy, and expect that a software release other than the one named may differ. What BMWeb itself will and will not write is in the documentation."},{"t":"Siemens MS45.1 DME","u":"ms45.html","x":"The engine computer of the 2003-on E46 six-cylinders. Its memory, how its boot loader takes a flash, what decides whether the result starts, what it checks about itself while it runs, and how it is married to the immobiliser."},{"t":"Siemens MS45.1 DME § Hardware","u":"ms45.html#hardware","x":"A Motorola MPC555 with an external AMD Am29BL802CB, a 1 MB bottom-boot flash on a 16-bit bus. The program lives across both: part in the MPC555's internal flash (448 KB), the rest and the calibration in the external chip. A small serial EEPROM on the MPC555's QSPI holds what the DME learns. RAM is 26 KB, not 32 The MPC555's SRAM is 0x3F9800 - 0x3FFFFF: 10 KB from 0x3F9800 and 16 KB from 0x3FC000. The 6 KB below it, 0x3F8000 - 0x3F97FF, is reserved in the user's manual and does not exist on the chip. The DME agrees three ways: the loader clears RAM from 0x3F9800, its RAM self-test covers 0x6800 bytes from there, and its diagnostic address table lists RAM as 0x3F9800 - 0x3FFFFF. firmware \"Unused\" RAM is not free on a running engine About 1.5 KB of RAM is never written once the program is running, as far as the code shows. Filling it with a pattern on a car stalled the engine at idle and reset the DME right after start-up, with faults for the engine speed limiter reset, the throttle start-up check and air temperature plausibility. The start and firing paths read RAM that the boot leaves initialised. car The external chip Sector layout from the datasheet, which is also how the DME is partitioned. Offsets are into the chip. firmware Offset Size Holds 0x00000 16 KB The reset vector at 0x100, the exception table and the first of the boot loader 0x04000 8 KB Boot loader: the hand-off to the program and the entry of its 9600 baud server, see below 0x06000 8 KB Boot loader 0x08000 96 KB Boot loader, including the flash drivers it copies to RAM, blank from 0x16934 to 0x1F7FF. At the top: the boot block checksum ( 0x1F800), the sector table ( 0x1F900), the immobiliser's ISN ( 0x1FB00) and the programming log ( 0x1FB34 up) 0x20000 128 KB More boot loader code (about 230 functions up to 0x37E8C), blank from about 0x38800. Erasing it destroys the loader. 0x40000 128 KB Calibration, 0x1D000 bytes of data, valid marks at the top 0x60000 128 KB Program 0x80000 256 KB Program 0xC0000 256 KB Program, valid marks at the top The chip is single-bank. While it erases or programs, every read returns status, so no code can run from it. Anything that writes the chip from the running program has to execute from SRAM or internal flash with interrupts off. Erase Suspend is ignored in burst mode, which the DME turns on at boot, so the chip cannot be erased with the engine running. firmware The boot loader is not one build. Two DMEs of the same part number differ in their boot blocks from 0x285B on; the flash driver inside is byte for byte the same but sits 0x24 further along in one of them. Code that looks for something in the boot region by address will find it in one unit and not the other. car The internal flash The MPC555's 448 KB is 14 blocks of 32 KB in two modules, programmed in 64-byte pages. The DME programs it with the chip maker's own driver, which the loader keeps in the 96 KB boot sector ( 0x14AFC - 0x162F8) and copies to RAM for the job. That driver programs one page in every selected block per pulse sequence: a page takes about 24.6 ms on silicon whether it is one block or twelve, so the same data goes in about four times faster when the blocks are written side by side. Programming needs the EPEE pin, which the loader raises on a MIOS port pin ( 0x306100 bit 0x40) before any flash work; without it the driver refuses with 0x87. car firmware The serial EEPROM A 4 KB (32 Kbit) part on QSPI chip select 1. Reading 64 KB of it gives the same 4 KB sixteen times, because the address wraps, so a write past 0xFFF lands on real data. car Read at boot, written in the after-run. The stored data (adaptions, faults, mileage, the immobiliser record) is read once at start-up and written back only after the ignition goes off, each record twice with a read-back: about 1200 writes on a blank part, 35 s of after-run. emulator Only the program sets the QSPI up. The boot loader never touches the QSPI module, so anything that runs before the program finds it at its reset state: slave mode, pins as plain I/O. A read started in that state waits for ever and the watchdog resets the DME. car \"RAM check\" means the EEPROM. Fault 2869 (P16A3, \"self-test: RAM check\") is set when the stored-data restore at boot fails: one block is kept three times and must agree two of three, another must hold a valid record. A blank or torn EEPROM fails it; a DME with its own EEPROM passes. It is not about the SRAM. emulator car"},{"t":"Siemens MS45.1 DME § The programming session","u":"ms45.html#session","x":"This is the order of jobs in a whole-program flash, from a real flasher log. The emulated DME answers every step the same way. car emulator 1A 89 read serial number 31 07 03 <4 bytes> read the seed 31 08 00 00 00 10 <key> send the key -> 71 08 01 accepted, 71 08 00 refused 10 85 05 programming session, 115200 baud 83 03 00 78 18 F0 00 timing: P2max 3 s, P3min 12 ms 31 02 06 00 00 02 0A 00 00 erase the program: 0x02060000 + 0xA0000 34.. 36.. 37 download the external program, 253-byte blocks 34 00 00 00 06 00 07 00 00 download the internal flash: 0x06000000 + 0x70000 31 02 04 00 00 02 02 00 00 erase the calibration: 0x02040000 + 0x20000 34 04 00 00 02 00 01 D0 00 download the calibration 10 81 01 default session, 9600 baud (the loader keeps running) 31 09 02 check the program signature -> 71 09 01 31 09 04 check the data signature -> 71 09 01 11 01 reset Addresses go as bytes 2, 1, 0, then 3. The external chip is 0x02000000 + offset and the internal flash 0x06000000 + offset. There is no separate erase for the internal flash: erasing the program region erases the external program sectors and all of the internal flash. emulator The erase is categorical. The range in the request is ignored: asked for the top 64 KB, the first 64 KB or the internal window, the loader wipes the same whole program area plus all of the internal flash every time. Only the calibration erase is its own category, so there is no partial program flash. emulator The key answer is 71 08 01 for accepted. A positive answer 71 08 00 means the key was refused, so checking only for 71 passes a wrong key. car The serial number used for the key is the last four bytes of the answer's payload, not of the telegram, which ends in a checksum. car Reading needs no unlock. A calibration read works in the default 9600 baud session. car The first erase hands over. The running program takes the first erase request, shuts itself down and jumps into the boot loader. That request is never answered; the next one is served by the loader. emulator The log entry comes last, and only then. The programming log write ( 3D) is accepted only after an erase and a download in the same programming session; at any other time it is refused with 7F 3D 22. A tool that only checks signatures can never add an entry. emulator Baud rate is a parameter The loader's 10 85 <code> takes codes 0-5 from a table: 2400, 9600, 19200, 38400, 57600, 115200. Any code of 6 or more is followed by a 24-bit baud rate, so 10 85 06 03 D0 90 asks for 250000, and the answer echoes it. The serial divider is round(1250000 / baud). The stock loader can therefore run any rate the line carries without a patch. emulator Timing What Value Gap before the next request (P3min) Exactly 12 ms. The loader refuses any other value with 7F 83 22, and a request sent sooner is silently dropped and only taken on the retry, which costs a whole answer timeout. emulator Erase About 10 s of 7F 31 23 (busy, ask again) before 71 02 01 car One 253-byte block at 19200 About 200 ms, occasionally 670 ms. A 500 ms timeout aborts a program flash part way. car One block at 115200 About 47 ms: 24.6 ms on the wire, about 10 ms in the loader, the answer and the 12 ms gap emulator Internal flash on silicon About 24.6 ms per 64-byte page when programmed one page at a time. Its driver can program the same page in every selected block in one pulse sequence, about 6 ms per page across 12 blocks. car Erasing the internal flash Under a second for any set of blocks, done as one job: 875 ms for one block, 956 ms for twelve. car A block answer is 76 <counter, 2 bytes> <status>, with status 01 for written and 02 for failed. Blank blocks can be skipped After an erase, downloading only the runs of bytes that are not FF leaves the chip identical to a full write. The signature check passes and the program starts. Runs may start at odd addresses and be a single block long. The stock calibration has 125 of 470 blocks blank at the end, the external program 535 of 2590. emulator The loader does not clamp a download to the erased sector A range that ran from the erased calibration across 0x5D000 into the unerased program was accepted and programmed into it. Programming only clears bits, so that corrupts the program. A tool that sends runs must clamp them to the region it erased. emulator Hand-off or park At every reset the loader runs first, from the vector at 0x100. About 117 µs in, it has checked the flash and takes one of two roads. emulator Hand-off. With a valid program and calibration it jumps to the program through one branch at 0x4EBC. Nothing else in the loader runs again until the next reset or the next programming session. Park. When the checks fail it waits a moment, then calls its 9600 baud server at 0x5228: an idle loop over a countdown table and a list of callbacks that answers identification, the status job and a new flash for as long as the power stays on. A valid boot never executes that call. Content decides, not the marks on their own. Blank valid marks with a good calibration still start the program, because the loader re-verifies the checksums; invalid content parks it. A read over diagnostics shows the marks as FF anyway, so a dump never tells you whether they are set. emulator car Parked, the loader answers a tester at 9600 like a running DME would: 3E (tester present), the hardware reference ( 22 25 02, \"0044570\" on this unit), the other reference numbers, and 31 0A with status 0C. A read of the programming log then comes back all FF if the top sector was erased. car A factory flash leaves the loader alone The whole-program session above writes the external program ( 0x60000 - 0xFFF3F), the internal flash and the calibration. Nothing in it touches the boot region below 0x40000, and nothing could: the loader's sector table at 0x1F900 gives the boot sector no write permission, so no erase or download request reaches it, whatever address it names. The only writes into that sector are the loader's own, its programming log entries. emulator firmware The other way round, code running on the DME itself can rewrite the boot sector: the chip has no hardware protection there. A power loss between erasing the 96 KB sector and writing it back leaves a DME that neither boots nor flashes, because the loader that would serve a new flash is what was erased. Only a hardware programmer brings such a unit back. emulator"},{"t":"Siemens MS45.1 DME § What makes a flash valid","u":"ms45.html#validity","x":"The loader computes checksums in the background, ten bytes per pass, both CRC-32/MPEG-2. emulator Program: over the internal flash and the external program, stored at 0x60000 and 0x60340. Calibration: over 0x40200 - 0x5CFFF, with start, end and initial value from the header at 0x40104, stored at 0x40100. The running program re-checks the same range in the background. 31 09 then checks the references and an RSA signature over the MD5 of the listed segments. On success it writes marks into the last bytes of the sector. emulator Complete Signature checked Program 0xFFF80 = 42902448 0xFFFC0 = 42244890 Calibration 0x5FF80 = 48249042 0x5FFC0 = 90482442 Status after a flash 31 0A reports where the loader stands. emulator Status Meaning 01 Normal 05 Program signature not checked 06 Data signature not checked 07 Interrupted, still in the programming session at 115200 and deaf to a tester at 9600 0C Program not complete, after an ignition cycle at 9600; a new flash is taken 0F The calibration's stored checksum does not match its content; 31 09 04 answers 71 09 00 A half-written program is recoverable: after an erase the DME stays in its boot loader and accepts a new program flash. car"},{"t":"Siemens MS45.1 DME § What the program checks about itself","u":"ms45.html#selftest","x":"Once running, the program keeps testing its own memory. Two of those tests matter to anyone who changes the flash. firmware emulator ROM test level 1: three checksums, including the boot block A background task walks a CRC-32/MPEG-2 over three ranges in turn, each described by a small table in the flash with its seed and stored word beside it. One full walk takes about 5 s. Phase Range Stored word Boot block External 0x0 - 0x1EFFF, from the words at 0x1F808 and 0x1F80C, seed at 0x1F9F0 0x1F800 Program Internal 0x0 - 0x6FFFF, then external 0x60608 - 0xFFF3F, header at 0x60004 - 0x60014 0x60000 Calibration From the table at 0x40108; skipped when the halfword at 0x40114 is blank in the header A changed boot block with an old checksum resets the car every 5 s Any phase failing sets the self-reset's code 11. The DME resets at the end of every walk and stores fault 28B2 (P16C3, \"engine speed limitation: reset\") with reason \"11 ROM Test Level 1\". A DME that ran fine on the bench and reset every 5-10 s in the car had exactly this: its boot region had been changed and the word at 0x1F800 had not. A program flash cannot cure it, because a program flash never writes the boot region; only rewriting the boot sector with a matching word does. The loader carries the same walker but boots the program with a stale word all the same, which is why the fault shows up only with the engine running. car The range excludes the top 2 KB of the sector: the sector table, the ISN and the programming log can change without touching the word. The safety monitor's own code sum (\"ROM test level 2\", stored at 0x60600) is a separate, simpler sum over the program and is what a patched program has to correct as well. Reset reasons The DME keeps a record of its own resets in RAM with a reason code. A reset without a record came from outside the program: the watchdog, or the monitoring module. Code 11 is the checksum walk above; the watchdog is serviced at the very end of the 10 ms task chain, so a watchdog reset means the chain did not finish, an unexpected exception or a jump into erased flash. A program patch that ran correctly at idle on the emulator and reset the real car at 3100-3400 rpm under load left no record: it ran its extra code from the external chip on paths the idle run never took, and the 10 ms budget was exceeded. car firmware"},{"t":"Siemens MS45.1 DME § The programming log","u":"ms45.html#aif","x":"Every successful flash leaves an entry (the AIF, in the diagnostic description's terms) in the tail of the boot sector, external 0x1FB34 - 0x1FFFF, in 64-byte slots. A slot is empty when its first byte is FF; every reader walks from the first slot and stops at the first empty one. firmware emulator 14 entries, not 19. 19 slots fit in the area, but the DME's log read (a window of service 23, what the description's AIF_LESEN uses) answers offsets below 0x380 and refuses the rest with 7F 23 52, even with 19 slots written. The description counts 14 free, and so does the DME. emulator The identity lives here. The VIN, the programming date, the software and assembly numbers that a tester shows for the DME come from the newest entry. A DME whose log is blank identifies with those fields empty, and its next entry gets software number 0000000, because the previous one was the only copy. car The boot block checksum excludes it, so entries can be added without changing anything else, which is how the loader itself adds them. Flash only clears bits: a change that only clears bits can be programmed over a slot in place, anything else means rewriting the sector. emulator A slot Decoded by running AIF_LESEN on marked slots; all fields round-trip. Offsets are into the slot. BMW data emulator Offset Field Form 0x01 - 0x07 VIN, the last 7 characters ASCII 0x09 - 0x0B Date BCD YY MM DD 0x0C - 0x11 Assembly number (ZB) BCD, 6 bytes 0x12 - 0x17 Software number BCD, 6 bytes 0x18 - 0x1D Approval (authority) number BCD, 6 bytes 0x1E - 0x20 Dealer number BCD 0x21 - 0x25 Tester serial ASCII 0x26 Mileage one byte, × 600 km 0x27 - 0x32 Programming reference ASCII 0x33 - 0x3C VIN, the first 10 characters ASCII"},{"t":"Siemens MS45.1 DME § The immobiliser","u":"ms45.html#ews","x":"The DME is married to the car's EWS3 by a 16-bit number, the ISN. The two talk over their own wire with a rolling code; the number itself never goes over the K line in plain. Found on three DMEs and settled by pairing two of them to one car. car firmware Where the ISN is In the external flash at 0x1FB00, as three transformed copies of the value V that the boot code votes on, after a marker halfword ( FEFE, ECEC or C8C8): Bytes Holds For V = A356 0-1 ~V, both bytes inverted 5C A9 2-3 V with the high byte inverted 5C 56 4-5 V with the low byte inverted A3 A9 Bytes 2-3 read raw give the wrong number, which is an easy mistake: only the low byte of that copy is straight. 0x1FB00 sits above the boot block checksum's range, so writing it changes no checksum. The EEPROM record is not the ISN The DME also keeps a 12-byte identity record in its EEPROM at 0x04 (a copy at 0x14): a 32-bit value and two masked copies of it. It is a per-unit number. Two DMEs with different values there both started the same car once their flash ISNs matched the EWS; the record never changed through pairing. Reading the ISN from the EEPROM gives a number that means nothing to the EWS. car The EWS3 will not tell you its ISN The EWS3 (DS2 address 0x44) answers identification (part 6905670) and its status, and refuses ISN_LESEN and PASSWORT_LESEN with A2 on a paired car with the ignition on. There is no precondition it waits for: in BMW's own description both jobs are one bare telegram each, with no login or state job in front. A paired EWS3 declines by policy, so the car's ISN has to come from its original DME, read from 0x1FB00 of a dump. car BMW data Pairing a used DME Write the car's ISN into the used DME's triple at 0x1FB00. Run the EWS start-value sync (the EWS3 description's WECHSELCODE_SYNC_DME and the DME's start-value jobs), which realigns the rolling code. The telegram to the EWS is 44 04 67; nothing secret crosses the K line. Ignition off for 20 s, then crank. The pairing is committed by a real start; it may take two attempts. The DME's lock flag (STATUS_EWS_LOCK, 21 49, LV_LOCK_IMOB) reads 1 right up until the engine actually starts. A 1 after a good sync is not a failure. Done twice with two different donor DMEs; the car started both times. car"},{"t":"Siemens MS45.1 DME § How fast the K line goes","u":"ms45.html#line","x":"With the DME's serial port set to each rate, over a K+DCAN cable on one car. car Baud Result 156250, 208333, 250000 Clean 312500 Clean in short tests, two garbled answers in about 300 during a long write 416667, 625000 The DME hears the tester, its answers arrive garbled The K-line transceivers documented for this era are rated to about 200 kBaud, so anything above that is outside spec and depends on the cable and the car. A line that is one-way at a high rate is a trap: the DME takes the request to switch and the tester never hears the confirmation, so a tool must fall back on its own when no answer comes."},{"t":"Siemens MS45.1 DME § Reading values while it runs","u":"ms45.html#live","x":"The stock program serves a logger in the default session, with no authentication. Measured on the emulated DME with the engine stopped. emulator Request Gives 10 81 05 The default session at 115200 baud ( 03 is 38400). Answered at the old rate, then the line switches. 83 03 04 01 18 14 00 Access timing at the session's limits: the DME answers after 2 ms instead of 25 and takes the next request 12 ms after its answer instead of about 27. Only exactly these values are accepted; anything else is 7F 83 22. 83 00 reads the limits, 83 01 goes back to the defaults. 2C F0 then 03 <pos> <size> <addr24> per piece Defines local identifier F0 from memory: up to 42 pieces in one request, pos the 1-based position in the packet. A second request replaces the first; 2C F0 04 clears it. 21 F0 The packet. 23 <addr24> <size> Memory: RAM from 0x3F9800 and the internal flash, up to 250 bytes. 251 to 254 get no answer at all; the external flash is refused with 7F 23 52. A 30-byte packet read back to back: 103 ms a read at 9600 (10 Hz), 57 ms at 115200 (17 Hz), 20 ms at 115200 with the timing set (50 Hz). About 9 ms is the floor; the diagnostic task comes round no sooner. The programming session has the same timing limits but refuses 21, 22, 2C and 18, so it is no use for logging. A real cable has to turn round in 2 ms for the fast timing: an FTDI adapter needs its latency timer at 1 ms, not the default 16."},{"t":"Siemens MS45.1 DME § The calibration definition","u":"ms45.html#xdf","x":"Checked by running the program and comparing every read it makes against the community XDF. emulator Coverage is complete. All 4,325 single-value reads land on a defined constant with the right start and size, and every table the lookup routines are handed is defined. One axis is missing: a 6-point 16-bit axis at calibration 0x8E30, used by the cruise control. 320 items have no description, 264 constants and 56 tables. One scaling looks wrong: c_temp_cur_on_ls_down is defined as 1/16 °C, but the variable it is compared with is 1/64 °C minus 33. Of 359 constants whose scale the code reveals, the other 354 agree."},{"t":"Siemens MS43 DME","u":"ms43.html","x":"The engine computer of the 2001-2003 E46 six-cylinders. This page covers its side of an automated gearbox: the 0x43D frame, fault 134, and how it learns which gearbox it has. Read from software MS430069 with a stock calibration."},{"t":"Siemens MS43 DME § CAN messages","u":"ms43.html#can","x":"The DME's message objects fix what it can hear. Anything else on the bus is ignored, under any name. firmware Direction Identifiers Receives 0x43F EGS1 (automatic), 0x613 and 0x615 (cluster), 0x153 and 0x1F3 (DSC), 0x43D AMT1 (automated manual), 0x1F5 (steering angle) Sends 0x316, 0x329, 0x545"},{"t":"Siemens MS43 DME § Which gearbox it thinks it has","u":"ms43.html#variant","x":"The DME is not coded for a gearbox and never reads the vehicle order. It learns the variant from the bus and keeps it. One bit is all diagnostics show. ECU_CONFIG's EGS_VORHANDEN and GE_VORHANDEN are the same bit (response byte 3, mask 0x10): a CAN gearbox is present. Nothing in the diagnostic description says whether it is an automatic or an automated manual, and no job writes it. BMW data With 0x43F on the bus it learns a CAN gearbox; take 0x43F away, clear the learned variants with ADAPT_VARIANTEN_LOESCHEN, and after a power cycle the bit reads 0 again. field report Once learned it is kept until ADAPT_VARIANTEN_LOESCHEN clears it. field report"},{"t":"Siemens MS43 DME § Fault 134","u":"ms43.html#fault134","x":"\"SSG-Eingriff unplausibel\" is three monitors under one code. The fault-type byte says which. BMW data Type Text OBD 0x7D SSG-Eingriff unplausibel: the frame's state is impossible P1670 0x7E SSG-Moment unplausibel: the torque request does not fit P1653 0x7F Timeout Message Counter: the alive counter stopped P1654 A missing 0x43D is a different fault, 129 \"Timeout SSG-Botschaft\". So 134 means the frame arrives and its content is rejected. The freeze frame stores STATE_AMT ( 0x8F), STATE_ERR_AMT ( 0x90), the DME's own torque TQI ( 0x91) and the gearbox's torque TQI_AMT ( 0x92). The two torques are stored at 4.01176 Nm per bit, which is the freeze frame's scale, not the frame's. BMW data STATE_ERR_AMT One routine writes it every 10 ms from the last 0x43D. The fault sets after about 0.2 s in an error state. firmware Value Cause 0x00 Plausible 0x01 No shift flagged, but STATE_AMT is not 0, or the torque byte is 10 Nm or more 0x11 Shift flagged with STATE_AMT 0 and byte 0 bit 7 clear 0x10 Shift flagged, STATE_AMT 0, bit 7 set, but torque above 0 or road speed above about 15 km/h 0x20 Shift state held too long: state 1 or 3 over 2.3 s, state 2 over 5.8 s 0x21 State 1 or 3: requested torque more than 60 Nm above actual for over 0.35 s 0x22 State 2: clutch field 3 for over 0.2 s 0x30 Byte 6 checksum wrong 0x40 Frame missing 0x50 Counter unchanged for 0.25 s Other values, such as 0x48, are not written by this routine."},{"t":"Siemens MS43 DME § The 0x43D frame","u":"ms43.html#frame","x":"As the DME reads it. firmware Byte Bits Meaning 0 0-2 Gear 0 3 LV_GS, shift in progress 0 5-6 STATE_AMT, the shift state (read as (byte0 >> 5) & 3) 0 7 Checked together with LV_GS, see 0x10 and 0x11 above 3 all Requested torque, absolute, 1.5625 Nm per bit (400 Nm full scale) 5 0 LV_GP, gearbox protection: invokes the road-speed limit c_vs_max_amt 5 2-5 Alive counter, one step per frame 5 6-7 Clutch state 6 all Checksum: 0xFF - min(0xFF, byte3 + counter) When no shift is in progress Every 10 ms. This clears fault 134 with the engine off and running. field report Byte 0: gear in bits 0-2, LV_GS 0, STATE_AMT 0. Byte 3: 0x00. A \"neutral\" 0xFF reads as about 398 Nm and gives a permanent 0x01. Byte 5: the counter in bits 2-5, the clutch state in bits 6-7. Byte 6: 0xFF minus the counter, since byte 3 is 0. During a shift Byte 0 = gear | 0x08 | (STATE_AMT << 5), with STATE_AMT 1, 2 or 3. LV_GS and STATE_AMT go up together and down together; LV_GS on its own is never valid. Byte 3 = the torque wanted, never more than 60 Nm above what the engine makes for longer than 0.35 s. Byte 6 recomputed every frame. Finish inside 2.3 s in states 1 and 3, 5.8 s in state 2, and in state 2 do not report clutch state 3 for more than 0.2 s."},{"t":"GS20 automatic gearbox","u":"gs20.html","x":"The control unit of the E46's five-speed GM automatic. A Siemens C167CR with an 8-bit external flash, programmed over DS2. Read from program 7552700 (release 90) and run in an emulator."},{"t":"GS20 automatic gearbox § Memory","u":"gs20.html#memory","x":"A 512 KB read is the boot block at 0x080000, the calibration at 0x090000 and the program at 0x0A0000. The flash is an Am29F400BB on an 8-bit bus. firmware The first 48 KB of the flash also appear at 0x000000. That is where the trap vectors are, so every interrupt enters the boot block first. The 1 ms tick and the K-line interrupts stay in the boot block whichever of boot block and program is running; the rest go to stubs in the program. Address bit 20 is ignored: the program sends its flash commands at 0x18xxxx, a byte at a time. The calibration carries a CRC-16, stored at 0x09000D."},{"t":"GS20 automatic gearbox § Identification","u":"gs20.html#ident","x":"A DS2 identification 32 04 00 36 is answered like this. A real module with the same software answered the same bytes on the bench except its production date and serial number. car emulator 32 2E A0 \"7552700\" \"29\" \"001060\" \"3905\" \"0000000000\" \"90\" \"10001399364\" B6 With the program region erased, the boot block answers the same telegram with the program's fields reading FF. Releases The boot block, K line and flash are the same for every release. Each of the thirteen programs in SP-Daten starts on the same boot block, with a calibration the boot block accepts for it, and runs without a fault. emulator Release Program 42 1423690 55 1423862 57 1423886 63 4455028 64 1423955, 7508145 74 7506226 76 7521759 85 7518709 86 7526396 88 7532988 89 7544721 90 7552700 Of the 219 calibrations, the boot block takes between one and 48 with each program."},{"t":"GS20 automatic gearbox § The programming session","u":"gs20.html#session","x":"Compared against the wire log of a real calibration write: the emulated module answers with the same bytes. car emulator No unlock for 10 s after power-up, and for 10 s after a wrong key. The answer while it waits is A2. The program answers the session open and hands over to the boot block at the first flash command. A module with no program is in its boot loader already and answers the open with A2. A write to a region not erased in this session is refused with B0, even if it is blank. A write over bytes already programmed is answered with sub-status 3. A blank program region gives validity code 0x0B, the flash status sub-code a flasher sees for it. The pace is the module's: it wants 25 ms of quiet before it takes a telegram. A calibration write is about 44 s of the module's time and a program write about 175 s, at 80 ms a chunk. The finish checks the pair. The end-of-programming command ( 07 0F) compares the calibration's ID blocks with the installed program's. A mismatch is answered with sub-status 0E and the module stays in programming state 3, so a calibration written onto a program it was not released for never completes. The programming record has its own window. The record area is 0x08F000 - 0x08FFFF, 46-byte records written in turn ( 0x08F000, 0x08F02E, 0x08F05C,...), read first and written after the data. It can only be written in programming state 1; in any other state the write is refused with B0. The factory flasher writes one record after the last file. BMW data"},{"t":"GS20 automatic gearbox § Why a bad program boot-loops","u":"gs20.html#bootloop","x":"The boot block checks a program's three ID blocks, not its code. A program with good IDs and broken code is started, crashes, is reset by the trap handler and started again, about every 70 ms, and never answers on the K line. emulator The boot loader's own loop, which is what takes a new program over the K line, only runs when the IDs do not match. Writing such a program goes through without complaint, because the session is served by the boot block and routines in RAM. It is the power cycle afterwards that bricks it. One way this happens An early patched build erased the last 32 bytes of three program segments. A routine near the end of one is called at start-up; the CPU ran through the FF s off the end of the segment, wrapped to the program header and hit an undefined opcode. The way back is the CPU's bootstrap loader in mask ROM, not the flash."},{"t":"GS20 automatic gearbox § The K-line driver","u":"gs20.html#kline","x":"The boot block's driver reads the RxD pin as well as the UART. While the pin is low it holds a 300 ms quiet timer and takes no telegram. It has both the 5-baud wake-up and KWP2000 built in. firmware"},{"t":"Diagnostics and the K line","u":"diagnostics.html","x":"How the E46-era units talk on the K line, what their answers mean, and what the cables and adapters in between can carry."},{"t":"Diagnostics and the K line § The line","u":"diagnostics.html#line","x":"The K line is one wire, half duplex. Everything a tester sends comes back to it as an echo before the unit answers, so a cable that does not hear its own echo is not on the line. The usual serial format is 8 data bits, even parity, 1 stop bit. An echo proves nothing about the car A USB K-line cable echoes every byte it sends with nothing plugged into it at all, at any rate from 9600 to 312500: the echo is the adapter's own loopback. Echo and no answer means no unit heard you. Check the plug and the key before suspecting a loader state or a baud rate. car"},{"t":"Diagnostics and the K line § DS2 telegrams","u":"diagnostics.html#ds2","x":"Used by the gearboxes, the cluster and most body modules. A telegram is the unit's address, the total length (address, length and checksum included), the data, and an XOR of every byte before it. car 32 04 00 36 GS20 (address 0x32), identification (00); 0x32 ^ 0x04 ^ 0x00 = 0x36 32 2E A0... B6 its answer; A0 = done Status Seen meaning A0 Done A2 Not now, or not for you. The GS20 says it for 10 s after power-up or a wrong key, and when it has no program and is already in its boot loader. The EWS3 (address 0x44) says it to ISN_LESEN and PASSWORT_LESEN on a paired car, ignition on, and there is no job that unlocks them: it declines by policy. See the immobiliser. B0 Refused. The GS20 says it for a write to a region not erased in this session, and for a programming record written outside the state that allows it. A positive answer can carry a sub-status after A0: the GS20 answers a write over bytes already programmed with sub-status 3, and the end of a programming whose calibration does not match the program with 0E. emulator"},{"t":"Diagnostics and the K line § KWP2000 telegrams","u":"diagnostics.html#kwp","x":"Used by the MS45.1 DME. A telegram is B8, the target, the source, a length byte, the data and a checksum. B8 is a fixed format byte, not 0x80 plus a length, so the length is always byte 3. The tester is F1 and the DME 12. car B8 12 F1 02 1A 89.. tester to DME: read the serial number B8 F1 12 03 7F 31 23.. DME to tester: routine not finished yet A positive answer is the request's service plus 0x40. A negative one is 7F, the service, and a reason: Reason Meaning What to do 23 Routine not complete Ask again. An erase says this for about 10 s. 78 Answer pending Wait. The answer follows on its own. 22 Conditions not correct A real refusal. An erase refuses this way when the supply voltage is too low; an access-timing request with any value but the unit's own limits; a programming log write outside a session that erased and downloaded something. 52 Address not served A memory read ( 23) of an address the unit does not hand out: the MS45.1's external flash, or its programming log past the 14th entry. 80 Refused A real refusal. Some answers are positive in form and still a refusal. The DME's key answer 71 08 00 means the key was rejected; only 71 08 01 unlocks it. See the MS45.1 session. Timing The unit's answer delay and the gap it wants before the next request are set by 83. 83 00 reads the limits, 83 03 sets values and 83 01 returns to the defaults. The MS45.1 accepts exactly its limits and nothing in between: its running program answers after 25 ms and wants about 27 ms of quiet by default, 2 ms and 12 ms at its limits ( 83 03 04 01 18 14 00); its boot loader wants 12 ms of quiet and gives itself 3 s to answer ( 83 03 00 78 18 F0 00). A request sent into the gap is silently dropped, not refused. emulator A memory read of 251 to 254 bytes gets no answer at all from the MS45.1, where 250 is served. Silence is a fourth kind of answer and a tool should time it out, not wait. emulator"},{"t":"Diagnostics and the K line § Cables and adapters","u":"diagnostics.html#adapters","x":"A K+DCAN USB cable carried the DME's programming session at 115200 and, with the DME's port set higher, up to 250000 cleanly and 312500 for short exchanges. Measurements. car WiFi adapters need care. One common adapter firmware only does parity between 980 and 25000 baud, and its 115200 mode has no parity. The DME's programming mode is 115200 with even parity, so its answers come back torn. Asking for programming mode at 19200 instead ( 10 85 02) works, and the DME keeps that rate through erase and write. 38400 and above did not. car Answer time grows with a slow line. At 19200 a flash block is usually answered in about 200 ms but occasionally in 670 ms, so block timeouts need to follow the negotiated P2max of 3 s, not a guess. car A fast turnaround needs a fast adapter. With the MS45.1's timing at its limits the tester has 2 ms to answer. An FTDI cable buffers received bytes for 16 ms by default; its latency timer has to be set to 1 ms. emulator A one-way line looks like a dead unit. At a rate the cable cannot carry back (416667 and up on the K+DCAN cable tried) the DME still hears the request to switch and switches, and its garbled answers never confirm it. Only an ignition cycle brings it back to 9600 unless the unit falls back on its own. car The transceivers. The MS43's K-line driver is an MC33199-class part rated to 200 kBaud; the MS45.1's could not be identified from the board. The MS45.1 carried 250000 and 312500 on one car, which says more about the margin in the parts than about any guarantee. car"},{"t":"Diagnostics and the K line § Flashing with BMW's own descriptions","u":"diagnostics.html#kfp","x":"BMW's flash descriptions for the DS2 units (the GS20 and SMG2 among 37) and the scripts that drive them were run offline against the emulated units. Three things in them are easy to get wrong. BMW data emulator The erase job has no timeout. Every DS2 flash description sets the standard timeout of FLASH_LOESCHEN to 0, which means no limit, not the default. A runtime that reads 0 as unset waits 2 s and gives up on an erase that takes longer; the flashing script then reports that failure as its battery-voltage error (2022), which is the script's own mapping of the job status, not a measurement. car One log record, after the last file. The factory flasher writes a programming record once, after the last of the files it programs, not after each one. A GS20 given a record after every file refuses the later ones. BMW data No script checks the battery or the ignition. None of the 522 flashing scripts reads the supply voltage or terminal 15. The checks a factory tool performs before a flash are the tool's, and an erase that the unit refuses for low voltage comes back as the unit's own answer ( 7F 31 22 from the MS45.1). BMW data"},{"t":"Coding","u":"coding.html","x":"How BMW's coding jobs talk to an E46 instrument cluster, what the factory coding tool does with the coding files, and the traps in them. Worked out by running BMW's own coding jobs and scripts offline against a simulated cluster, reading the factory tool, then on a car."},{"t":"Coding § The E46 cluster on the wire","u":"coding.html#cluster","x":"The cluster is a DS2 unit at address 0x80. car Telegram data Does 00 Identification. The coding index is the 6th data byte of the answer. 06 03 00 hi lo n Read n bytes of coding memory 07 03 00 hi lo n data Write them 12 Reset, so the cluster takes the new coding The checksum is an XOR over the coding words, stored in its own word through a mask. The factory job computes it; a value worked out the same way matches the one stored in a real cluster. BMW data The write job reads its own write back and fails with ERROR_VERIFY when the bytes differ. BMW data The reset clears the clock. After coding, the display showing only a blank clock is the reset, not a coding fault. car A real write Changing the temperature unit from °F to °C on an order-coded car took 16 write telegrams through the factory script: the service-data groups, then the coding words 12 at a time, the checksum word, the reset, and a read to prove it. car"},{"t":"Coding § Key-coded and order-coded cars","u":"coding.html#scripts","x":"A late E46 cluster is coded through the KOMBI46R description with one of two scripts, and the car decides which. BMW data Key-coded cars use A_KMB46. Order-coded cars use A_AKMB46. Their cluster's key-code block is blank FF with check characters. car The cluster script always takes its \"not yet coded\" branch, because the status word it tests includes the checksum byte. With a plant number other than 0000 it also runs the job C_ZEIT_RESET, so a tool should send 0000 unless it means that. An order's time criterion picks the variant. In the order-coding tables, rows marked Z or A #MMYY carry a date, and continue on lines starting >>>. A 09/04 car resolves its cluster to coding index C08, KOMBI46R, A_AKMB46."},{"t":"Coding § Coding files","u":"coding.html#files","x":"The file extension is the coding index in hex. Index 52 is LSZ.C34. Diagnostic results give the index in decimal. BMW data Masks are per byte. A function's mask has one entry for each byte it touches. Treating it as one mask for the whole value produced 233 false bit collisions across the files. BMW data Two-byte values are held by the module in address order. Parsing their hex as one number swaps the bytes. car String positions in BMW's scripts count from 0: midstr(text, 0, 2) is the first two characters. BMW data"},{"t":"Coding § How a coding job is asked","u":"coding.html#jobs","x":"The coding jobs (C_S_LESEN, C_C_LESEN, C_S_AUFTRAG and their relatives) take one buffer, and the module's job code reads it by offset. Measured by running the jobs, and read from the factory tool that builds it. BMW data Offset Holds 0 Type: 1 for whole words (the mask all FF), 2 for one word with a partial mask 1 Word width, 1, 2 or 4 bytes 2, 3 Byte order and address mode 9 up The mask, one byte per byte of the word 0x0D Byte count, 16 bit (the body module's jobs read this) 0x0F Word count, 16 bit (the cluster's jobs read this) 0x11 Word address, 32 bit, little endian: the byte address divided by the word width, with the block number in the upper bytes 0x15 Data, then a closing 03 The answer echoes the header. A read comes back as the same 21-byte header, the data and 03. A buffer one byte short of that is answered ERROR_BIN_BUFFER. Type 2 is for clusters only. Of 313 coding job sets, only the cluster ones (C_KMB*, KOMBI46R, KOMBI85, C_SHD) accept a partial-mask word; the rest answer ERROR_DATA_TYPE. Everything else is written as whole words, which is why the factory tool reads the module first. Blocks are the address's upper bytes. A module that organises its coding memory in blocks (the body module, the light module, the parking aid, the airbag module) gets the block number folded into the word address: width × block × 256, so a 16-bit address overflows for the higher blocks. Each module's jobs pick the block out of the byte they expect (most from byte 0x12 of the buffer, the ALC from two). The old 09 <address / 20> write job only serves the body module's C01/C02 variants, whose coding is one 20-byte region; a C05/C06 car codes its body module through the ZKE5_S12 jobs. BMW data Some blocks are the manufacturer's. A module refuses a coding job on its HERSTELLER and RESERVIERT blocks with ERROR_ECU_PARAMETER."},{"t":"Coding § What the factory tool writes","u":"coding.html#factory","x":"Read from the factory coding tool itself, against the E46 coding files. BMW data The written set is the OR of every active function's mask with the UNBELEGT1 fill rows ( UNBELEGT2 gives the fill value). A byte whose mask is all set is sent as data, a partial one as a type-2 word, a clear one not at all. 115 of the 129 E46 descriptions come out fully covered, all type 1. What it never writes: the cluster's coding date, VIN, key-code block and mileage bytes, two equipment bytes, and the airbag module's VIN and manufacturer data. A tool that writes the whole region sends them back as the module holds them. Values. A reset codes every function to its active value. A function with no decision and one possible value gets it; with several the tool stops with an error. A condition table (CVT) is strict: exactly one rule per group must hold, later entries overwrite earlier ones. Order to keywords. The option table (AT) has rows typed W, E, H, K, Z and A; Z and A #MMYY rows carry a date and continue on >>> lines, and the order's time criterion picks among them. In the ZST table a module-field value is matched nibble by nibble with 0 as a wildcard, and an all-zero row matches everything. The condition expression grammar is NOT above AND above OR,, for OR and + for AND. The module's selection table (SGET) takes the first row whose conditions hold. Its S<id> entries are equipment keyword ids (on the E46, S37 is AUTOMATIK, S121 KMBI_CI_07)."},{"t":"Coding § Not settled yet","u":"coding.html#open","x":"Coding a block-addressed module with the rules above has been run offline against the jobs, not yet on a car. The equipment filter, which hides functions a car's options do not have, needs the option ids measured on a car. The option table's V and N dates and its = conditions are read approximately; whether a date on such a row means on-or-after or before has not been checked against a car."}];
